use Libraries.Web.Page.all
use Libraries.Web.Page.Standards.ComputerScienceStandards2026
use Libraries.Web.Page.Standards.QuorumStandards

/*
    Tutorial: Option Dialogs, ready-made dialogs that ask one yes or no
    question, in the user interface series.
*/
class UserInterfacesOptionDialog is QuorumPage
    // The site's sets of standards, built once for every page.
    QuorumStandards standards
    // A double quote, for code that holds one.
    text dq = ""

    on create
        SetLocation("tutorials/ui/optionDialog.html")
        SetShortTitle("Option Dialogs")
        SetSectionName("User Interfaces")
    end

    action Setup
        dq = dq:GetDoubleQuote()

        // For search engines and link previews.
        SetDescription("Ask a yes or no question with a Quorum OptionDialog, react to Yes and No, use it with a keyboard, and learn how fake warning dialogs trick people.")

        Title("Option Dialogs")

        Overview()
        WarmUp()
        Explore()
        Engage()
        WrapUp()
    end

    action Overview
        Section("Overview")
        Paragraph("Many programs need to ask a quick yes or no question before they do
something: "):DoubleQuote():Text("Delete this file?"):DoubleQuote():Text(", "):DoubleQuote():Text("Quit without saving?"):DoubleQuote():Text(", "):DoubleQuote():Text("Start a new game?"):DoubleQuote():Text("
Quorum has a ready-made dialog for this, called an "):Strong("option dialog"):Text(". It
shows one question with a Yes button and a No button. You don't have to
build its layout. You give it the question and tell it what to do when the
person picks Yes. In this tutorial, you will ask a question with an
"):Code("OptionDialog"):Text(" from a button on a page, react to Yes and to No, and test
what the dialog is like to use with a keyboard.")

        Subsection("Goals")
        Paragraph("You have the following goals for this tutorial:")
        Goals()
        ListItem("Create an "):Code("OptionDialog"):Text(" and set its question with "):Code("SetLabelTitle")
        ListItem("Run your own code when the person picks Yes, with "):Code("SetBehavior")
        ListItem("Run your own code when the person picks No, with "):Code("SetCancelBehavior")
        ListItem("Explain when a program should ask before it acts")
        ListItem("Recognize how a fake warning dialog tricks people, and design honest ones")
    end

    action WarmUp
        Section("Warm up")
        Paragraph("Programs ask "):DoubleQuote():Text("Are you sure?"):DoubleQuote():Text(" all the time. Think of one time this saved
you from a mistake, and one time it just got in your way. What was
different about the two? Make a short list of actions that "):Strong("should"):Text(" ask
first, and actions that should "):Strong("not"):Text(".")

        Subsection("Vocabulary")
        Paragraph("You will be learning about the following vocabulary words:")
        Table("Vocabulary", "media/userInterface/OptionDialog/Vocabulary.csv")

        Subsection("New Code to Learn")
        Paragraph("You will be using the following new pieces of code:")
        Table("New Code to Learn", "media/userInterface/OptionDialog/Code.csv")

        Subsection("CSTA Standards")
        Paragraph("This tutorial covers the following "):Link("CSTA standards", "https://csteachers.org/pk12standards/view/"):Text(":")
        ComputerScienceStandards2026 computerScience = standards:GetComputerScience2026()
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:HIGH_SCHOOL_TESTING_AND_REFINING_20)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:MIDDLE_SCHOOL_TESTING_AND_REFINING_20)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:SPECIALTY_1_SOFTWARE_DEVELOPMENT_USER_EXPERIENCE_5)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:MIDDLE_SCHOOL_SECURITY_33)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:HIGH_SCHOOL_SECURITY_32)
        StandardsList(computerScience)
    end

    action Explore
        Section("Explore")

        WhatAnOptionDialogIs()
        AskingAQuestion()
        DoingSomethingOnNo()
        UsingTheKeyboard()
        WhenToUseAnOptionDialog()
        DialogsThatTrickPeople()
    end

    private action WhatAnOptionDialogIs
        Subsection("What an option dialog is")
        Paragraph("An "):Code("OptionDialog"):Text(" is a kind of "):Code("Dialog"):Text(" that Quorum has already built for
you. (The "):Link("Dialogs tutorial", "/tutorials/ui/dialog.html"):Text(" shows how to build
your own.) It comes with:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem("a question, which says "):DoubleQuote():Text("Are you sure?"):DoubleQuote():Text(" until you change it;")
        ListItem("a "):Strong("No"):Text(" button, which closes the dialog;")
        ListItem("a "):Strong("Yes"):Text(" button, which runs the behavior you give it;")
        ListItem("a top bar with the dialog's name and an X button.")

        Paragraph("It is always "):Strong("modal"):Text(": while it is open, the rest of the program ignores
the mouse and keyboard.")
    end

    private action AskingAQuestion
        Subsection("Asking a question")
        Paragraph("In this program, a "):DoubleQuote():Text("New Game"):DoubleQuote():Text(" button on the page asks before it sets the
score back to 0.")

        Paragraph("First, add a class named "):Code("YesBehavior.quorum"):Text(". It runs when the person
picks Yes. It sets the score label back to 0, then closes the dialog.")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/ui/OptionDialog/YesBehavior.quorum")

        Paragraph("The Yes button does "):Strong("not"):Text(" close the dialog on its own. Your behavior has
to call "):Code("Hide"):Text(", or the dialog stays open.")

        Paragraph("Now replace "):Code("Main.quorum"):Text(":")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/ui/OptionDialog/Main.quorum")

        Paragraph("Here is what is new:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem():Code("Main"):Text(" is the New Game button's behavior, as in the
"):Link("Forms", "/tutorials/ui/forms.html"):Text(" tutorial. Its "):Code("Run"):Text(" action opens the
question.")
        ListItem():Strong("The dialog is made inside "):Code("Run"):Strong("."):Text(" An option dialog gets its drawing
ready as soon as it is made, and that only works once the app's window is
open. "):Code("Run"):Text(" happens when someone presses the button, which is always
after "):Code("form:Display()"):Text(". The "):Code("if question = undefined"):Text(" check makes the
dialog only the first time, and keeps it in "):Code("question"):Text(" to reuse.")
        ListItem():Code("SetLabelTitle(" + dq + "Start a new game?" + dq + ")"):Text(" sets the question. It also names the
dialog "):DoubleQuote():Text("Start a new game? Dialog"):DoubleQuote():Text(", which is shown in the top bar and read
by a screen reader.")
        ListItem():Code("SetDescription"):Text(" adds more detail for screen reader users, so they know
what Yes and No will do.")
        ListItem():Code("SetBehavior(yesBehavior)"):Text(" connects your "):Code("YesBehavior"):Text(" to the Yes button.")

        Paragraph("Run the program and press New Game. Pick No, and the score stays at 50.
Press New Game again and pick Yes, and the score goes to 0.")
    end

    private action DoingSomethingOnNo
        Subsection("Doing something when the person picks No")
        Paragraph("By default, No only closes the dialog. If your program needs to do
something on No, write a behavior and give it to "):Code("SetCancelBehavior"):Text(". Add a
class named "):Code("NoBehavior.quorum"):Text(":")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/ui/OptionDialog/NoBehavior.quorum")

        Paragraph("Then add these lines to "):Code("Run"):Text(" in "):Code("Main"):Text(", right after
"):Code("dialog:SetBehavior(yesBehavior)"):Text(":")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/ui/OptionDialog/NoLines.quorum")

        Paragraph("Just like Yes, once you replace the No behavior, your behavior must call
"):Code("Hide"):Text(".")

        BlockEditorFile("YesBehavior.quorum", "html/sources/tutorials/ui/OptionDialog/YesBehavior.quorum")
        BlockEditorFile("NoBehavior.quorum", "html/sources/tutorials/ui/OptionDialog/NoBehavior.quorum")
        BlockEditor("Main.quorum", "html/sources/tutorials/ui/OptionDialog/Main2.quorum", "", true, true)
    end

    private action UsingTheKeyboard
        Subsection("Using the keyboard")
        Paragraph("When the dialog opens, Quorum puts the focus on the Yes button. Here is what
the keys do:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem():Key("Tab"):Text(" moves between Yes and No.")
        ListItem():Key("Space"):Text(" or "):Key("Enter"):Text(" presses the button that has the focus.")
        ListItem():Key("Escape"):Text(" closes the dialog "):Strong("without"):Text(" running either behavior. Treat
"):Key("Escape"):Text(" the same as No: your program should not change anything.")

        Paragraph("When the dialog closes, the focus goes back to the New Game button.")

        Paragraph("Because the focus starts on Yes, a person who presses "):Key("Enter"):Text(" right away
picks Yes. That is fine for "):DoubleQuote():Text("Start a new game?"):DoubleQuote():Text(", but think carefully before
using an option dialog for something that can't be undone, like deleting a
file.")
    end

    private action WhenToUseAnOptionDialog
        Subsection("When to use an option dialog")
        Paragraph("An option dialog is a good choice when:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem("the question has two answers, yes or no;")
        ListItem("the action is hard to undo, like starting over or deleting;")
        ListItem("the person might have chosen the action by mistake.")

        Paragraph("It is a poor choice when the action is small or easy to undo. Asking too
often teaches people to press Yes without reading. If you need different
answers, more text, or other controls, build your own dialog with a page,
as shown in the "):Link("Dialogs tutorial", "/tutorials/ui/dialog.html"):Text(".")
    end

    private action DialogsThatTrickPeople
        Subsection("Dialogs that trick people")
        Paragraph("People trust dialogs, because a dialog seems to come from the computer
itself. Attackers use that trust. Tricking a person into doing something
unsafe, such as paying, downloading a program or typing a password, is
called "):Strong("social engineering"):Text(". One kind uses fake warnings, called
"):Strong("scareware"):Text(": a message that claims your computer has a problem, so you
will pay for a fix you don't need.")

        Paragraph("In 2008, the US Federal Trade Commission (FTC) took a group of companies to
court for scareware. The FTC said that the companies placed ads that sent
people to web sites claiming to scan their computers. The scans reported
viruses, spyware and errors that weren't there, and urged people to buy
security programs for $39.95 or more. The FTC said more than one million
people were tricked. The ads also fooled the web sites that showed them,
since the companies pretended to buy ad space for real businesses. A court
ordered the companies to stop making the false claims
("):Link("source", "https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/news/press-releases/2008/12/court-halts-bogus-computer-scans"):Text(").")

        Paragraph("Here is a made-up fake warning, written for this tutorial:")
        Callout("Made-up fake warning")
        Paragraph():Strong("WARNING!"):Text(" 37 viruses found! Your files will be deleted in 60 seconds.
Press Yes to fix your computer now.")
        EndCallout()

        Paragraph("Notice the tricks: a scary claim with an exact number, a countdown that
rushes you, and a Yes button that only leads to a payment. An honest dialog
does the opposite. It says exactly what will happen, it doesn't invent a
deadline, it makes No as easy to pick as Yes, and its name matches the
program that opened it. To protect yourself, don't trust a warning that
appears inside a web page or an ad. Close it, and check with the security
program that is really installed on your computer.")
    end

    action Engage
        Section("Engage")
        Paragraph("In this activity, you and a partner change an option dialog so it asks a
clear question before an action that can't be undone. The goal is to decide
when a program should ask first, and to write a question whose answer is
obvious. Start with the program above. Then pick an action from your Warm
up list that "):Strong("should"):Text(" ask first, and change the program so the button, the
question and the description all match that action. Change what
"):Code("YesBehavior"):Text(" does to fit.")

        Paragraph("Have your partner use the program with only the keyboard, and ask them: did
they know what Yes would do before they pressed it? Then rewrite the
question so the answer is clear even without the description. For example,
"):DoubleQuote():Text("Delete my_story.txt?"):DoubleQuote():Text(" is clearer than "):DoubleQuote():Text("Are you sure?"):DoubleQuote()

        Paragraph("Next, each of you writes, on paper or with physical manipulatives, a made-up
fake warning in the style of the scareware example, with at least two tricks
in it. Swap them.
Find every trick in your partner's warning, and rewrite it as an honest
dialog. Then debate it together: in the FTC case, who was harmed, and how?
Sort your answers into people, businesses, and governments, and agree on
one way to prevent each harm.")
    end

    action WrapUp
        Section("Wrap up")
        Paragraph("An option dialog is a small speed bump. When does a speed bump protect
people, and when does it only slow them down? Who should decide which
actions get one: the programmer, or the person using the program? A fake
warning and a real one can use the same dialog. What makes one honest?")
    end
end
